{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Correction: two things I asserted in the plan were WRONG, and the guards caught both\n\nRecording these because I stated them as design facts in the plan and in an earlier comment,\nand they did not survive contact with the actual guards.\n\n**1. I claimed a metric-math alarm is \"excluded by construction from the top-level-namespace\nguards, as `monitoring_eb6e.tf`'s Bedrock alarm is.\" That is false.** The guards read\n`namespace` with a MULTILINE regex, which matches the value nested inside a `metric_query`\nblock exactly as readily as a top-level one. The Bedrock alarm is excluded because its\nnamespace VALUE is `AWS/Bedrock` — not because its declaration is nested. My alarm names\n`rebar/host`, so every host-alarm invariant applies to it. It now satisfies them rather than\nopting out, and the corrected reasoning is written into the file for the next person who\nassumes what I did.\n\n**2. `treat_missing_data = \"notBreaching\"` on the dead-man is forbidden by invariant I3, and\nthat is a real constraint, not a technicality.** A non-breaching alarm must budget >= 600s of\npublisher time per REQUIRED datapoint; 36 required datapoints in a 10800s window budgets 300s\neach. The only way to keep `notBreaching` would be dropping to <= 18-of-36 — but M-of-N counts\nANY 18 buckets in the window rather than a consecutive run, and my own measurement (49 of 72\nbuckets at 1 during ordinary truncation) says that alarm would fire more or less continuously.\n\nSo the dead-man is `breaching` with M == N == 36. Its condition is now a SUPERSET of the\nexpression: 36 consecutive periods that are each either \"memory absent while the probe ran\"\n(expression = 1) or \"neither metric published\" (missing = a dead probe). The second case is\nalso `mcp_serving_path_down`'s, so this can double-page one outage. That cost is accepted and\nrecorded rather than engineered around, because the alternative is letting a dead publisher\nread as health. The 36-period run-length semantics the window was measured for are preserved.\n\n**3. `terraform validate` rejected a top-level `period` on the metric-math alarm** —\n\"conflicts with metric_query\". Removed; the 300s cadence lives in the metric blocks, which is\nalso where the window guards read it from, so `period x evaluation_periods` still evaluates to\nthe intended 10800s window. This is the one thing in this change that only an actual\n`terraform validate` could have caught, which is why it was run rather than assumed.\n\n## Verification performed\n\nRED before GREEN on every test, then each fix PERTURBED to prove the test fails for the\nproperty it names — 14 perturbations, all of which failed exactly their named assertion and\nrestored green:\n\n- compose: drop mcp's limit; set mcp below its measured need; trim gerrit below its config\n  reservation; raise review-bot past the recorded over-subscription ceiling.\n- gate admission: remove the floor call; invert the comparison so low memory admits; make the\n  ABSENT branch emit a disarm; make the UNREADABLE branch silent; set `rebar.toml` back to 4.\n- parity oracle: drift compose's `mem_limit` without moving `--memory`; drop `--memory`.\n- alarms: dead-man M<N; strip the `notBreaching` opt-out marker; drop `alarm_actions`; delete\n  the whole alarm file (anti-vacuity floors).\n\nThe `gate_host` fixture now supplies a synthetic `/proc/meminfo`. Without that, the 27\npre-existing tests in that file would have started depending on how much memory the CI runner\nhappened to have free — green on a fat box, red on a loaded one, for reasons unrelated to the\nproperty under test. That is a flakiness vector this change would otherwise have introduced.\n\n`terraform validate` PASSES. Terraform is written and validated but **NOT applied**."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788663762756409001,"uuid":"e6d96ab4-b21a-4889-8f6b-cab1016cabf1"}